  {
    "id" : "DL3000",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce use of absolute WORKDIR",
    "description" : "This rule enforces the use of absolute paths in the WORKDIR instruction in Dockerfiles.",
    "explanation" : "## Use absolute WORKDIR.\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM busybox\nWORKDIR usr/src/app\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM busybox\nWORKDIR /usr/src/app\n```\n### Rationale:\n\nBy using absolute paths you will not run into problems when a previous `WORKDIR` instruction\nchanges. You also often times don't know the `WORKDIR` context of your base container.\n\n### Exceptions:\n\nWhen using environment replacements.\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM busybox\nENV foo /bar\nWORKDIR ${foo}   # WORKDIR /bar\n```\n\n### Contraindications\n\nWORKDIR is [not recommended](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/creating-actions/dockerfile-support-for-github-actions#workdir) in GitHub actions.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3001",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Running Inappropriate Commands in Docker Containers",
    "description" : "This rule disallows running certain commands in Docker containers that are either bound to the host system or are interactive utilities that do not make sense within a container environment.",
    "explanation" : "## Command does not make sense in a container.\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM busybox\nRUN top\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM busybox\n```\n### Rationale:\n\nFor some POSIX commands it makes no sense to run them in a Docker container because they are bound to the host or are otherwise dangerous (like ´shutdown´, ´service´, ´ps´, ´free´, ´top´, ´kill´, ´mount´, ´ifconfig´).\nInteractive utilities also don't make much sense (´nano´, ´vim´).",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3002",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "Security",
    "subCategory" : "InsecureModulesLibraries",
    "title" : "Avoid using root as the last USER in Dockerfile",
    "description" : "Ensure that the last user in the Dockerfile is not root to reduce security risks.",
    "explanation" : "## Last user should not be root.\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM busybox\nUSER root\nRUN ...\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM busybox\nUSER root\nRUN ...\nUSER guest\n```\n### Rationale:\n\nSwitching to the root `USER` opens up certain security risks if an attacker gets access to the container. In order to mitigate this, switch back to a non privileged user after running the commands you need as root.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3003",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Use WORKDIR to Switch to a Directory",
    "description" : "Enforce using the WORKDIR instruction to change directories instead of using 'cd' within RUN commands in Dockerfiles.",
    "explanation" : "## Use absolute paths, or use WORKDIR to switch to a directory.\n\n### Problematic code #1:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM busybox\nRUN cd /usr/src/app && git clone git@github.com:lukasmartinelli/hadolint.git \n```\n\n### Correct code #1 with `WORKDIR` (when current working directory should be changed):\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM busybox\nWORKDIR /usr/src/app\nRUN git clone git@github.com:lukasmartinelli/hadolint.git\n```\n\n### Problematic code #2:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM busybox\nRUN cd /usr/src/app && cp somedir/somefile ./someDirInUsrSrcApp/\n```\n\n### Correct code #2 with absolute paths (when current working directory should _not_ be changed):\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM busybox\nRUN cp somedir/somefile /usr/src/app/someDirInUsrSrcApp/\n```\n\n### Rationale:\n\nOnly use `cd` in a subshell. Most commands can work with absolute paths and it in most cases not necessary\nto change directories. Docker provides the `WORKDIR` instruction if you really need to change the current\nworking directory, but please mind its \"side effects\" (see below for more details).\n\n### Exceptions:\n\nWhen executed in a Subshell.\n\nPlease note that there may be side effects by the last `WORKDIR` instruction, as this is also (a) the current\nworking directory when the Docker container is finally started, or (b) the working directory for any `RUN`,\n`CMD`, `ENTRYPOINT`, `COPY `and `ADD `instructions that follow it in the Dockerfile (see [Dockerfile `WORKDIR`\ndocumentation](https://docs.docker.com/reference/dockerfile/#workdir)). Moreover using `cd` in a `RUN`\ninstruction also the advantage of being free of side effects afterwards: that is, it only affects this single\n`RUN` instruction, but not any subsequent instructions.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3004",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Using sudo in Dockerfiles",
    "description" : "This rule prohibits the use of sudo in Dockerfiles because it can cause unpredictable behavior.",
    "explanation" : "## Do not use sudo.\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM busybox\nRUN sudo apt-get install\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM busybox\nRUN apt-get install\n```\n### Rationale:\n\nDo not use sudo as it leads to unpredictable behavior. Use a tool like gosu to enforce root.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3005",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Using apt-get dist-upgrade",
    "description" : "This rule forbids the use of 'apt-get dist-upgrade' in Dockerfiles to prevent potential issues with container image consistency and reliability.",
    "explanation" : "## Do not use apt-get dist-upgrade.\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM debian\nRUN apt-get update && apt-get dist-upgrade \n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM debian\nRUN apt-get update && apt-get upgrade\n```\n\n### Rationale:\n\n([rationale is outdated](https://github.com/hadolint/hadolint/issues/562))\n\n~http://crosbymichael.com/dockerfile-best-practices-take-2.html~\n\n> ~Updates will be baked into the base images, so you don't need to run `apt-get upgrade` in your containers. Because of the isolation that happens this can often fail if something is trying to modify init or make device changes inside a container. It also produces inconsistent images because you no longer have one source of truth of how your application should run and what versions of dependencies are included in the image.~",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3006",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Explicit Version Tag on Docker Image",
    "description" : "This rule enforces that every Docker image reference includes an explicit version tag rather than relying on implicit or latest tags.",
    "explanation" : "## Always tag the version of an image explicitly.\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM debian\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM debian:jessie\n```\n### Rationale:\n\nYou can never rely that the `latest` tags is a specific version.\n\nhttps://docs.docker.com/engine/userguide/dockerimages/\n\n> Tip: You recommend you always use a specific tagged image, for example ubuntu:12.04. That way you always know exactly what variant of an image is being used.\n\n### Exceptions:\n\nWhen the image name refers to a previously defined alias:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM debian:jessie as build\n\nRUN build_script\n\nFROM build as tests\n\nRUN test_script\n\nFROM debian:jessie\n\nCOPY --from=build foo .\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3007",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Using 'latest' Tag in Docker Images",
    "description" : "This rule enforces pinning Docker image versions explicitly instead of using the 'latest' tag.",
    "explanation" : "## Using latest is prone to errors if the image will ever update. Pin the version explicitly to a release tag.\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM debian:latest\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM debian:jessie\n```\n### Rationale:\n\nYou can never rely that the `latest` tags is a specific version.\n\nhttps://docs.docker.com/engine/userguide/dockerimages/\n\n> Tip: You recommend you always use a specific tagged image, for example ubuntu:12.04. That way you always know exactly what variant of an image is being used.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3008",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Pinning Versions in apt-get install",
    "description" : "This rule enforces pinning specific versions when using apt-get install in Dockerfiles to ensure consistent builds.",
    "explanation" : "## Pin versions in apt get install.\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM busybox\nRUN apt-get install python\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM busybox\nRUN apt-get install python=2.7\n```\n### Rationale:\n\nhttps://docs.docker.com/develop/develop-images/instructions/#apt-get\n\n> Version pinning forces the build to retrieve a particular version regardless of what’s in the cache. This technique can also reduce failures due to unanticipated changes in required packages.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3009",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Delete apt-get lists after installing packages",
    "description" : "Ensure that apt-get lists are deleted after installing packages to reduce Docker image size.",
    "explanation" : "## Delete the apt-get lists after installing something.\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```dockerfile\nRUN apt-get update && apt-get install --no-install-recommends -y python\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```dockerfile\nRUN apt-get update && apt-get install --no-install-recommends -y python \\\n && apt-get clean \\\n && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*\n```\n\n### Rationale:\n\nhttps://docs.docker.com/develop/develop-images/instructions/#apt-get\n\n> In addition, when you clean up the apt cache by removing /var/lib/apt/lists it reduces the image size, since the apt cache isn’t stored in a layer. Since the RUN statement starts with apt-get update, the package cache is always refreshed prior to apt-get install.\n\n### Notes:\n\nClean up must be performed in the same RUN step, otherwise it will affect image size.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3010",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Use ADD for Extracting Archives into an Image",
    "description" : "This rule enforces using the ADD instruction instead of COPY when extracting archive files into a Docker image.",
    "explanation" : "## Use ADD for extracting archives into an image.\n\n### Rationale:\n\nhttps://docs.docker.com/develop/develop-images/instructions/#add-or-copy\n\n> Although ADD and COPY are functionally similar, generally speaking, COPY is preferred. That’s because it’s more transparent than ADD. COPY only supports the basic copying of local files into the container, while ADD has some features (like local-only tar extraction and remote URL support) that are not immediately obvious. Consequently, the best use for ADD is local tar file auto-extraction into the image, as in ADD rootfs.tar.xz /.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3011",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce valid UNIX port range in Dockerfile EXPOSE instruction",
    "description" : "This rule ensures that the port number specified in the EXPOSE instruction of a Dockerfile is within the valid UNIX port range of 0 to 65535.",
    "explanation" : "## Valid UNIX ports range from 0 to 65535.\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM busybox\nEXPOSE 80000\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM busybox\nEXPOSE 65535\n```\n### Rationale:\n\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_TCP_and_UDP_port_numbers",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3012",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Multiple HEALTHCHECK Instructions in Dockerfile",
    "description" : "This rule prevents using more than one HEALTHCHECK instruction per Dockerfile stage.",
    "explanation" : "## Multiple `HEALTHCHECK` instructions\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM busybox\nHEALTHCHECK CMD /bin/healthcheck\n[...]\nHEALTHCHECK CMD /bin/something\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM busybox\nHEALTHCHECK CMD /bin/healthcheck\n```\nor\n```dockerfile\nFROM busybox\nHEALTHCHECK NONE\n```\n### Rationale:\n\nProviding more than one `HEALTHCHECK` instruction per stage is confusing, error prone and possibly makes for larger than necessary Docker images.\n\n### Note:\nThis rule used to be `Provide an email adress or URL as maintainer.`, but that was deprecated and eventually replaced with the current rule.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3013",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Pin Versions in pip Installations",
    "description" : "Ensure that pip package installations in Dockerfiles specify explicit version numbers or commit hashes to avoid unintentional upgrades.",
    "explanation" : "## Pin versions in pip.\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM python:3.4\nRUN pip install django\nRUN pip install https://github.com/Banno/carbon/tarball/0.9.x-fix-events-callback\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM python:3.4\nRUN pip install django==1.9\nRUN pip install git+https://github.com/Banno/carbon@0.9.15\n```\n### Rationale:\n\nhttps://docs.docker.com/develop/develop-images/instructions/#apt-get\n\n> Version pinning forces the build to retrieve a particular version regardless of what’s in the cache. This technique can also reduce failures due to unanticipated changes in required packages.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3014",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Use of -y Switch in apt-get Install",
    "description" : "Ensures the use of the '-y' switch with 'apt-get install' commands in Dockerfiles to automatically confirm prompts.",
    "explanation" : "## Use the -y switch.\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM debian\nRUN apt-get install python=2.7\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM debian\nRUN apt-get install -y python=2.7\n```\n### Rationale:\n\nWithout the `--assume-yes` option it might be possible that the build breaks without human intervention.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3015",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Use of --no-install-recommends with apt-get install",
    "description" : "This rule enforces specifying the --no-install-recommends option when using apt-get install to avoid installing additional, unnecessary packages.",
    "explanation" : "## Avoid additional packages by specifying --no-install-recommends.\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM busybox\nRUN apt-get install -y python=2.7\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM busybox\nRUN apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends python=2.7\n```\n\n### Rationale:\n\nAvoid installing additional packages that you did not explicitly want.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3016",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Pin Versions in npm Install",
    "description" : "Ensure that npm packages are installed with pinned versions in Dockerfiles to avoid unexpected changes and build failures.",
    "explanation" : "## Pin versions in `npm`.\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM node:8.9.1\n\nRUN npm install express\nRUN npm install @myorg/privatepackage\nRUN npm install express sax@0.1.1\nRUN npm install --global express\nRUN npm install git+ssh://git@github.com:npm/npm.git\nRUN npm install git+http://isaacs@github.com/npm/npm\nRUN npm install git+https://isaacs@github.com/npm/npm.git\nRUN npm install git://github.com/npm/npm.git\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM node:8.9.1\n\nRUN npm install express@4.1.1\nRUN npm install @myorg/privatepackage@\">=0.1.0\"\nRUN npm install express@\"4.1.1\" sax@0.1.1\nRUN npm install --global express@\"4.1.1\"\nRUN npm install git+ssh://git@github.com:npm/npm.git#v1.0.27\nRUN npm install git+http://isaacs@github.com/npm/npm#semver:^5.0\nRUN npm install git+https://isaacs@github.com/npm/npm.git#v1.0.27\nRUN npm install git://github.com/npm/npm.git#v1.0.27\n```\n### Rationale:\n\nhttps://docs.docker.com/develop/develop-images/instructions/#apt-get\n\n>Version pinning forces the build to retrieve a particular version regardless of what’s in the cache. This technique can also reduce failures due to unanticipated changes in required packages.\n\n### Exceptions:\n\nPin your versions in `package.json` and run `npm install` with no arguments.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3018",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Pin Versions in apk add",
    "description" : "Enforce pinning package versions when using apk add in Dockerfiles to ensure consistent builds.",
    "explanation" : "## Pin versions in `apk add`\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM alpine:3.7\nRUN apk --no-cache add foo\n```\n\n### Correct code (Partial pin glob):\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM alpine:3.7\nRUN apk --no-cache add foo=~1.2.3\n```\n\n### Correct code (Exact pin):\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM alpine:3.7\nRUN apk --no-cache add foo=1.2.3\n```\n\n**Note:** Pinning exact versions can cause future builds to suddenly fail if that version is no longer available. Use with caution. \n\nAvailable versions in apk can search on <https://pkgs.alpinelinux.org>\n\n### Rationale:\n\nhttps://docs.docker.com/develop/develop-images/instructions/#run\n\n> Version pinning forces the build to retrieve a limited range of versions, or an exact particular version, regardless of what’s in the cache. This technique can also reduce failures due to unanticipated changes in required packages.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3019",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Use --no-cache Switch for apk Package Installation",
    "description" : "Enforce using the `--no-cache` switch with apk to avoid caching package indexes and unnecessary cache cleanup steps.",
    "explanation" : "## Use the `--no-cache` switch\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM alpine:3.7\nRUN apk update \\\n    && apk add foo=1.0 \\\n    && rm -rf /var/cache/apk/*\n```\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM alpine:3.7\nRUN apk add --update foo=1.0\n    && rm -rf /var/cache/apk/*\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM alpine:3.7\nRUN apk --no-cache add foo=1.0\n```\n### Rationale:\n\nAs of Alpine Linux 3.3 there exists a new `--no-cache` option for `apk`. It allows users to install packages with an index that is updated and used on-the-fly and not cached locally:\n\nThis avoids the need to use `--update` and remove `/var/cache/apk/*` when done installing packages.\n\n> inspired by https://github.com/gliderlabs/docker-alpine/blob/master/docs/usage.md#disabling-cache",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3020",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Use COPY Instead of ADD for Files and Folders",
    "description" : "This rule enforces the use of COPY instead of ADD when copying files and folders in Dockerfiles.",
    "explanation" : "## Use COPY instead of ADD for files and folders\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM python:3.4\nADD requirements.txt /usr/src/app/\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM python:3.4\nCOPY requirements.txt /usr/src/app/\n```\n### Rationale:\n\nhttps://docs.docker.com/develop/develop-images/instructions/#add-or-copy\n\n> For other items (files, directories) that do not require ADD’s tar auto-extraction capability, you should always use COPY.\n\nRule also implemented in https://github.com/RedCoolBeans/dockerlint/blob/master/src/checks.coffee\n\n### Exceptions:\n\n> Consequently, the best use for ADD is local tar file auto-extraction into the image.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3021",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce COPY Destination Ends with Slash for Multiple Sources",
    "description" : "Ensures that when the COPY instruction in a Dockerfile has more than two arguments, the last argument (destination) ends with a slash '/' to indicate a directory.",
    "explanation" : "## COPY with more than 2 arguments requires the last argument to end with /\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM node:carbon\nCOPY package.json yarn.lock my_app\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM node:carbon\nCOPY package.json yarn.lock my_app/\n```\n### Rationale:\n\nhttps://docs.docker.com/engine/reference/builder/#copy\n\n> If multiple <src> resources are specified, either directly or due to the use of a wildcard, then <dest> must be a directory, and it must end with a slash /",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3022",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce COPY --from References Defined FROM Alias",
    "description" : "Ensures that the COPY --from instruction in a Dockerfile references a previously defined FROM alias.",
    "explanation" : "## COPY --from should reference a previously defined FROM alias\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM debian:jesse\n\nRUN stuff\n\nFROM debian:jesse\n\nCOPY --from=build some stuff ./\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM debian:jesse as build\n\nRUN stuff\n\nFROM debian:jesse\n\nCOPY --from=build some stuff ./\n```\n### Rationale:\n\nTrying to copy from a missing image alias results in an error.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3023",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid COPY --from referencing its own FROM alias",
    "description" : "Disallow the use of COPY --from to reference the same build stage alias as the current FROM instruction in Dockerfiles.",
    "explanation" : "## COPY --from cannot reference its own FROM alias\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM debian:jesse as build\n\nCOPY --from=build some stuff ./\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM debian:jesse as build\n\nRUN stuff\n\nFROM debian:jesse\n\nCOPY --from=build some stuff ./\n```\n### Rationale:\n\nTrying to copy from the same image the instruction is running in results in an error.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3024",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Unique FROM Aliases (Stage Names) in Dockerfiles",
    "description" : "Ensure that each FROM alias (stage name) in a Dockerfile is unique to avoid build errors.",
    "explanation" : "## FROM aliases (stage names) must be unique\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM debian:jesse as build\n\nRUN stuff\n\nFROM debian:jesse as build\n\nRUN more_stuff\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM debian:jesse as build\n\nRUN stuff\n\nFROM debian:jesse as another-alias\n\nRUN more_stuff\n```\n### Rationale:\n\nDefining duplicate stage names results in an error.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3025",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Use JSON Array Notation for CMD and ENTRYPOINT Arguments",
    "description" : "Enforce the use of JSON array notation for CMD and ENTRYPOINT instructions in Dockerfiles instead of plain string notation.",
    "explanation" : "## Use arguments JSON notation for CMD and ENTRYPOINT arguments\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM busybox\nENTRYPOINT s3cmd\n```\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM busybox\nCMD my-service server\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM busybox\nENTRYPOINT [\"s3cmd\"]\n```\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM busybox\nCMD [\"my-service\", \"server\"]\n```\n\n**Warning**: Docker `CMD` does not process `$ENVIRONMENT_VARIABLE`s, that’s a side-effect of using `sh -c` as the default entry-point. Using the JSON notation means that you have to figure out how to handle environment variables yourself.\n\n**Warning**: The CMD exec form is parsed as a JSON array, so you **MUST** use double quotes (`\"`) instead of single quote (`'`). See https://docs.docker.com/v17.09/engine/reference/builder/#cmd for more info.\n\n### Rationale:\n\nWhen using the plain text version of passing arguments, signals from the OS are not correctly passed to the executables, which is in the majority of the cases what you would expect.\n\nhttps://docs.docker.com/engine/articles/dockerfile_best-practices/\n\n> `CMD` should almost always be used in the form of `CMD [“executable”, “param1”, “param2”…]`\n\n> The shell form prevents any CMD or run command line arguments from being used, but has the disadvantage that your ENTRYPOINT will be started as a subcommand of /bin/sh -c, which does not pass signals. This means that the executable will not be the container’s PID 1 - and will not receive Unix signals - so your executable will not receive a SIGTERM from docker stop <container>.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3026",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "Security",
    "subCategory" : "InsecureModulesLibraries",
    "title" : "Enforce Use of Allowed Registry in FROM Image",
    "description" : "Ensure that Docker images in the FROM instruction are pulled only from trusted registries.",
    "explanation" : "## Use only an allowed registry in the FROM image\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM randomguy/python:3.6\n...\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM my-registry.com/python:3.6\n...\n```\n\n### Rationale:\n\nUsing the `FROM` instruction is a huge exercise in trust, you have to trust that a particular version or an image is safe for you to use, and that it will never be retagged maliciously. In order to prevent that, some companies copy trusted images into their own repositories, and reference them directly.\n\nFor example, this would be an untrusted image:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM randomguy/fancy:10\n...\n```\n\nBut after an audit, the company decides to copy the image into their own repository, as it was deemed safe:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM my-registry.com/fancy:10\n...\n```\n\nThe idea is that hadolint can warn whenever an untrusted repo is used, you can use the `--trusted-registry` flag for that\n\n```bash\nhadolint --trusted-registry my-registry.com Dockerfile\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3027",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Using apt in Dockerfiles; Use apt-get or apt-cache Instead",
    "description" : "This rule enforces not using the 'apt' command in Dockerfiles, recommending 'apt-get' or 'apt-cache' instead.",
    "explanation" : "## Do not use apt as it is meant to be an end-user tool, use apt-get or apt-cache instead.\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM busybox\nRUN apt install curl=1.1.0\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM busybox\nRUN apt-get install curl=1.1.0\n```\n### Rationale:\n\n`apt` is discouraged by the linux distributions as an unattended tool as its interface may suffer changes between versions. Better use the more stable `apt-get` and `apt-cache`",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3028",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Pin Versions in gem install",
    "description" : "Ensure gem install commands specify explicit version numbers to avoid unexpected version changes.",
    "explanation" : "## Pin versions in gem install\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM ruby:2\nRUN gem install bundler\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM ruby:2\nRUN gem install bundler:1.1\n```\n### Rationale:\n\nhttps://docs.docker.com/engine/articles/dockerfile_best-practices/\n\n> Version pinning forces the build to retrieve a particular version regardless of what’s in the cache. This technique can also reduce failures due to unanticipated changes in required packages.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3029",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Using --platform Flag with FROM Instruction",
    "description" : "This rule prohibits the use of the --platform flag in the Dockerfile FROM instruction.",
    "explanation" : "# **Do not use --platform= with FROM.**\n\n## **Problematic code:**\n\n`FROM --platform=x86 busybox`\n\n## **Correct code:**\n\n`FROM busybox`\n\n## **Rationale:**\n\nSpecifying --platform= in the [docker file FROM clause](https://docs.docker.com/engine/reference/builder/#from) forces the Image to build only one target platform. This has a number of negative consequences:\n* It is not possible to build a [multi-platform Image](https://www.docker.com/blog/docker-official-images-now-multi-platform/) from this Docker file.\n* The platform that you build on must be the same as the platform specified in `--platform=`\n\nA better approach is to omit `FROM --platform` in the docker file and to specify in `buildx --platform=` during the build if control of the image platform target is needed.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3030",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce using -y switch with yum install",
    "description" : "Ensure the yum install command uses the -y switch to avoid manual input during package installation.",
    "explanation" : "## Use the `-y` switch to avoid manual input `yum install -y <package>`\n\n### Problematic code:\n```Dockerfile\nFROM centos\nRUN yum install httpd-2.24.4 && yum clean all\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n```Dockerfile\nFROM centos\nRUN yum install -y httpd-2.24.4 && yum clean all\n```\n\n### Rationale:\nWithout the `-y` flag or the equivalent `--assumeyes` flag, yum will not successfully install a package, because human input is expected. The `-y` flag makes yum assume 'yes' as the answer to prompts during the installation.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3032",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce yum clean all after yum command",
    "description" : "Ensures that 'yum clean all' is run after any yum command to clean cached package data and reduce Docker image size.",
    "explanation" : "## `yum clean all` missing after yum command.\n\n### Problematic code:\n```Dockerfile\nRUN yum install -y httpd-2.24.2\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n```Dockerfile\nRUN yum install -y httpd-2.24.2 && yum clean all\n```\n\n### Rationale:\nClean cached package data after installation to reduce image size.\n\nhttps://docs.docker.com/engine/articles/dockerfile_best-practices/\n\n### Notes:\nClean up must be performed in the same RUN step, otherwise it will not affect image size.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3033",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Specify Version When Installing Packages with yum",
    "description" : "Enforces specifying the exact version of packages when using 'yum install -y' in Dockerfiles.",
    "explanation" : "## Specify version with `yum install -y <package>-<version>`\n\n### Problematic code:\n```Dockerfile\nRUN yum install -y httpd && yum clean all\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n```Dockerfile\nRUN yum install -y httpd-2.24.2 && yum clean all\n```\n\n### Rationale:\n> Version pinning forces the build to retrieve a particular version regardless of what’s in the cache. This technique can also reduce failures due to unanticipated changes in required packages.\n\nhttps://docs.docker.com/develop/develop-images/dockerfile_best-practices/\n\n### Notes:\nIf you need downgrade an already installed package to an older version, use `yum downgrade -y <package_name>-<version_info>`",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3034",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Non-Interactive Switch in zypper Install Commands",
    "description" : "Ensure that the zypper install command includes the non-interactive switch (-y or --no-confirm) to avoid build failures.",
    "explanation" : "## Non-interactive switch missing from `zypper` command: `zypper install -y`\n\n### Problematic code:\n```Dockerfile\nRUN zypper install httpd=2.4.46 && zypper clean\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n```Dockerfile\nRUN zypper install -y httpd=2.4.46 && zypper clean\n```\n\n### Rationale:\nOmitting the non-interactive switch causes the command to fail during the build process, because zypper would expect manual input. Use the `-y` or the equivalent `--no-confirm` flag to avoid this.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3035",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Using zypper dist-upgrade",
    "description" : "This rule disallows the use of 'zypper dist-upgrade' or blanket 'zypper update' commands in Dockerfiles for openSUSE images to prevent unreliable and inconsistent updates.",
    "explanation" : "## Do not use `zypper update`\n\n### Problematic code:\n```Dockerfile\nFROM opensuse/leap:15.2\nRUN zypper update -y\n```\n### Correct code:\n```Dockerfile\nFROM opensuse/leap:15.2\nRUN zypper install -y httpd\\>=2.4 && zypper clean\nRUN zypper patch --cve=cve-2015-7547 && zypper clean\n```\n### Rationale:\n\nSee [DL3031](https://github.com/hadolint/hadolint/wiki/DL3031), [DL3005](https://github.com/hadolint/hadolint/wiki/DL3005). Problems include:\n\n  * Updates failing on packages from base images in unprivileged containers\n  * Inconsistencies between builds producing problems for application developers\n\n### Notes:\nThis rule lints against blanket updates and dist-upgrades, but allows more specific updates by two methods:\n\n  * use `zypper install -y $PACKAGE>=$VERSION` to upgrade a particular package, giving a version requirement.\n  * use `zypper patch` to mitigate particular security vulnerabilities.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3036",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce zypper clean after zypper use",
    "description" : "Ensures that 'zypper clean' is run after using 'zypper' to delete unneeded caches and reduce image size.",
    "explanation" : "## `zypper clean` missing after zypper use\n\n### Problematic code:\n```Dockerfile\nFROM opensuse/leap:15.2\nRUN zypper install -y httpd=2.4.46\n```\n### Correct code:\n```Dockerfile\nFROM opensuse/leap:15.2\nRUN zypper install -y httpd=2.4.46 && zypper clean\n```\n### Rationale:\nReduce layer and image size by deleting unneeded caches after running `zypper`.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3037",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Version Pinning with zypper install",
    "description" : "This rule enforces specifying package versions when using 'zypper install -y' in Dockerfiles to ensure consistent builds.",
    "explanation" : "## Specify version with `zypper install -y <package>[=]<version>`\n### Problematic code:\n```Dockerfile\nFROM opensuse/leap:15.2\nRUN zypper install -y httpd && zypper clean\n```\n### Correct code:\n```Dockerfile\nFROM opensuse/leap:15.2\nRUN zypper install -y httpd=2.4.46 && zypper clean\n```\n### Rationale:\n> Version pinning forces the build to retrieve a particular version regardless of what’s in the cache. This technique can also reduce failures due to unanticipated changes in required packages.\n(https://docs.docker.com/develop/develop-images/dockerfile_best-practices/)\n\n### Notes:\nZypper also supports version constraining:\n```Dockerfile\nRUN zypper install -y httpd\\>=2.4 && zypper clean\n```\nUse this to specify minimum version requirements.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3038",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Use of -y Switch in dnf install",
    "description" : "Ensures the use of the '-y' flag with 'dnf install' commands to prevent manual input prompts during Docker image builds.",
    "explanation" : "## Use the `-y` switch to avoid manual input `dnf install -y <package>`\n\n### Problematic code:\n```Dockerfile\nFROM fedora:32\nRUN dnf install httpd-2.4.46 && dnf clean all\n```\n### Correct code:\n```Dockerfile\nFROM fedora:32\nRUN dnf install -y httpd-2.4.46 && dnf clean all\n```\n### Rationale:\nOmitting the non-interactive switch causes the command to fail during the build process, because `dnf` would expect manual input. Use the `-y` or the equivalent `--assumeyes` flag to avoid this.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3040",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce dnf clean all After dnf Command",
    "description" : "Ensures that 'dnf clean all' is run after any 'dnf' command in a Dockerfile to clean cached package data.",
    "explanation" : "## `dnf clean all` missing after `dnf` command.\n### Problematic code:\n```Dockerfile\nRUN dnf install -y httpd-2.24.2\n```\n### Correct code:\n```Dockerfile\nRUN dnf install -y httpd-2.24.2 && dnf clean all\n```\n### Rationale:\n\nClean cached package data after installation to reduce image size.\n\nhttps://docs.docker.com/engine/articles/dockerfile_best-practices/\n### Notes:\n\nClean up must be performed in the same `RUN` step, otherwise it will not affect image size.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3041",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Version Pinning with dnf install",
    "description" : "This rule enforces specifying a package version when using 'dnf install -y' in Dockerfiles to ensure consistent builds.",
    "explanation" : "## Specify version with `dnf install -y <package>-<version>`\n### Problematic code:\n```Dockerfile\nRUN dnf install -y httpd && dnf clean all\n```\n### Correct code:\n```Dockerfile\nRUN dnf install -y httpd-2.24.2 && dnf clean all\n```\n### Rationale:\n\n> Version pinning forces the build to retrieve a particular version regardless of what’s in the cache. This technique can also reduce failures due to unanticipated changes in required packages.\n\nhttps://docs.docker.com/develop/develop-images/dockerfile_best-practices/\n### Notes:\n\nIf you need downgrade an already installed package to an older version, use `dnf downgrade -y <package_name>-<version_info>`",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3042",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Using pip Cache Directory with pip install",
    "description" : "This rule enforces the use of the --no-cache-dir option when running pip install in Dockerfiles to avoid caching package files.",
    "explanation" : "## Avoid cache directory with `pip install --no-cache-dir <package>`\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```dockerfile\nRUN pip3 install MySQL_python\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```dockerfile\nRUN pip3 install --no-cache-dir MySQL_python\n```\n### Rationale:\n\nOnce a package is installed, it does not need to be re-installed and the Docker cache can be leveraged instead. Since the pip cache makes the images larger and is not needed, it's better to disable it.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3043",
    "level" : "Error",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid ONBUILD Triggering ONBUILD, FROM, or MAINTAINER",
    "description" : "Disallow the use of ONBUILD instructions that trigger ONBUILD, FROM, or MAINTAINER commands within a Dockerfile.",
    "explanation" : "# ONBUILD, FROM or MAINTAINER triggered from within ONBUILD instruction.\n\n### Problematic code:\n```Dockerfile\nONBUILD ONBUILD /bin/true\nONBUILD FROM debian\nONBUILD MAINTAINER Ron Weasley\n```\n\n### Correct code:\nNone.\n```Dockerfile\n```\n\n### Rationale:\nThese three cases are errors in Dockerfiles. See:\nhttps://docs.docker.com/engine/reference/builder/#onbuild",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3044",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid referring to environment variables within the same ENV statement where they are defined",
    "description" : "This rule ensures that environment variables are not referenced within the same ENV instruction where they are declared in Dockerfiles.",
    "explanation" : "# Do not refer to an environment variable within the same `ENV` statement where it is defined.\n\n### Problematic code:\n```Dockerfile\nENV FOO=bar \\\n    BAZ=${FOO}/bla\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n```Dockerfile\nENV FOO=bar\nENV BAZ=${FOO}/bla\n```\n\n### Rationale:\nDocker will not expand a variable within the same `ENV` statement where it is defined. While it will not crash Docker, it carries a high likelihood of errors.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3045",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid COPY to Relative Destination Without WORKDIR Set",
    "description" : "Detects COPY commands in Dockerfiles that use a relative destination path without a WORKDIR being set.",
    "explanation" : "# `COPY` to a relative destination without `WORKDIR` set.\n\n### Problematic code:\n```Dockerfile\nFROM scratch\nCOPY foo bar\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n```Dockerfile\nFROM scratch\nCOPY foo /bar\n```\nor\n```Dockerfile\nFROM scratch\nWORKDIR /\nCOPY foo bar\n```\n\n### Rationale:\nWhile `COPY`ing to a relative path is not problematic per sé, errors happen, when changes are introduced to the `WORKDIR` without updating the destination of the `COPY` command. Since it is easy to overlook this relationship, Hadolint emits this warning when no `WORKDIR` is set and `COPY` has a relative destination. This case is error prone and either setting the `COPY`-destination absolute or the `WORKDIR` explicitly will reduce the probability of having an error.\nIt is assumed, that when a `WORKDIR` is set, the programmer will make sure it works well together with the destination of the `COPY` statements.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3046",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce useradd with -l flag for high UID",
    "description" : "Checks that the useradd command uses the -l flag when creating users with high UIDs to prevent excessive image size inflation.",
    "explanation" : "# `useradd` without flag `-l` and high UID will result in excessively large Image\n\n### Problematic code:\n```Dockerfile\nRUN useradd -u 123456 foobar\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n```Dockerfile\nRUN useradd -l -u 123456 foobar\n```\n\n### Rationale:\nWithout the `-l` or the `--no-log-init` flag, `useradd` will add the user to the lastlog and faillog databases. This can result in the creation of logically large (sparse) files under `/var/log`, which in turn unnecessarily inflates container image sizes. This is due to the lack of support for sparse files in overlay filesystems. For what it's worth, this behavior becomes more apparent with longer UIDs, resulting in a few megabytes of extra image size with a six digit UID, up to several gigabytes of excessive image size with even longer UIDs.\nDisabling this functionality from `useradd` has minimal disadvantages in a container but saves space and build time.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3047",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce wget Progress Flag Usage",
    "description" : "Ensure wget commands include the --progress flag to prevent excessively bloated build logs when downloading large files.",
    "explanation" : "## Use `wget --progress` to avoid excessively bloated build logs\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM ubuntu:20\nRUN wget https://example.com/big_file.tar\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM ubuntu:20\nRUN wget --progress=dot:giga https://example.com/big_file.tar\n```\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM ubuntu:20\nRUN wget -nv https://example.com/big_file.tar\n```\n\n### Rationale:\n\n`wget` without flag `--progress` will result in excessively bloated build logs when downloading larger files. That's because it outputs a line for each fraction of a percentage point while downloading a big file.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3048",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Valid Label Key",
    "description" : "This rule checks that Dockerfile LABEL keys use valid strings according to Docker's official label key specifications.",
    "explanation" : "## Invalid Label Key\n\n### Problematic code:\n```Dockerfile\nLABEL +?not..valid--key=\"foo\"\n```\n### Correct code:\n```Dockerfile\nLABEL valid-key.label=\"bar\"\n```\n\n### Rationale:\nNot all strings are supported as label keys. See [the official documentation](https://docs.docker.com/config/labels-custom-metadata/) for reference.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
