  {
    "id" : "DL3056",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Semantic Versioning in Label",
    "description" : "Ensures that the 'version' label in a Dockerfile conforms to semantic versioning standards.",
    "explanation" : "## Label `<label>` does not conform to semantic versioning.\n\nThis rule is activated by specifying a label-schema including a label of type `semver`. This can be done via command line\n```bash\n$ hadolint --require-label version:semver Dockerfile\n```\nor in the config file:\n```yaml\nlabel-schema:\n  version: semver\n```\n\n### Problematic code:\n```Dockerfile\nLABEL version=\"something-other-than-a-version-number\"\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n```Dockerfile\nLABEL version=\"1.0.0-rc1\"\n```\n\n### Rationale:\nSpecifying a version either for the resulting image or for the software contained within is often a crucial part of release engineering. [Semantic versioning](https://semver.org/) allows to do so in a well understood manner, which also communicates extend of changes between versions or possible incompatibilities.\n\n### Note:\nThis rule does not expand variables. It requires the value of a label to be a literal semantic version number.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3057",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce HEALTHCHECK Instruction Presence",
    "description" : "Ensures that a Dockerfile includes a HEALTHCHECK instruction to define container health monitoring.",
    "explanation" : "## `HEALTHCHECK` instruction missing.\n\nThis is an optional rule which is disabled by default. To enable it, set its severity to the preferred level, e.g.\n```Bash\n$ hadolint --warning=DL3057 Dockerfile\n```\n\n### Problematic code:\n```Dockerfile\nFROM busybox\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n```Dockerfile\nFROM busybox\nHEALTHCHECK CMD /bin/health\n```\nor\n```Dockerfile\nFROM busybox\nHEALTHCHECK NONE\n```\n\n### Rationale:\nWhen it is required to define a health check (e.g. by company policy), it must not be omitted.\n\n### Note:\nThis rule is disabled by default, because a `HEALTHCHECK` is not desirable in all circumstances. Images used with Kubernetes for example do not benefit from a `HEALTHCHECK` instruction, as Kubernetes brings its own mechanisms to accomplish the same thing. If however it is desired, this rule makes sure a build stage either has a `HEALTHCHECK` instruction, or inherits it from an earlier build stage.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "DL3058",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Valid Email Format in Label",
    "description" : "Ensures that labels of type email conform to the RFC 5322 email format standard.",
    "explanation" : "## Label `<label>` is not a valid email format - must be conform to RFC5322.\nThis rule is activated by specifying a label-schema including a label of type email. This can be done via command line\n```bash\n$ hadolint --require-label maintainer:email Dockerfile\n```\nor in the config file:\n```yaml\nlabel-schema:\n  maintainer: email\n```\n### Problematic code:\n```Dockerfile\nLABEL maintainer=\"foo-bar\"\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n```Dockerfile\nLABEL maintainer=\"foo@bar.com\"\n```\n\n### Note:\nThis rule does not expand variables. It requires the value of a label to be a literal email compliant with [RFC 5322](https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc5322).",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
